Part 2 of 3 for #117 (freshness): devices keep, carry and show publishers' signed statuses. Part 1 (#137) added the format. Part 3 has lychnion-data sign a status with every catalog; until then no status exists, and nothing new shows.

Never a block: old data keeps working. The app only says when something newer exists, or when its news is old.

**Kept on the device** (`lib/download/status_list.dart`, new):
- the newest status of each publisher whose key the device trusts, in `downloads/status.json` (`format: 1`);
- checked again with the current keys on every read, and left out of use once its key is revoked;
- a status signed more than a day after the reader's clock never decides. `PublisherStatus.deciding` applies this on the device, on sticks and on nodes, so a stolen key's status dated far ahead cannot push the real one out;
- trusted ones are filtered before picking the newest, and a kept status with a revoked key no longer decides. So the real replacement is kept, also when it arrives in the same catalog as the thief's later-dated one.

**Revocations and withdrawals travel inside:**
- `Catalog.parse` checks a catalog's statuses after its key statements. The key statements and withdrawals inside **every** status (within the scan limits) join the catalog's own: they count for that catalog's records at once, and are kept and passed on. A stick load and a node pull do the same.
- A kept status is read again when the device lost some of what it carried (`knownIds`).

**Passed on:** in the catalog written to a stick (an old stick's far-future status left out), in a base's catalog (cache key includes statuses; `base_status_left_out` log), and by nodes (`NodeFolder.status`, `writeCatalog(status:)`, pull, and a CLI line).

**Shown in Downloads:**
- under an item: "A newer version 2026.11.10 exists, but it is not available here yet", when the publisher's status names a newer version than both the one offered and the one installed;
- at the top: "Latest news from Lychnion: <date>";
- after 30 days without a newer status from Lychnion, a card: "The latest news from Lychnion on this device is from <date>. Connect to check for updates when you can.";
- nothing before any status has arrived.

Docs:
- help en/ja `04-downloads.md`, saying statuses come with an upcoming data release (help database rebuilt);
- docs/distribution.md;
- CHANGELOG (Security, Formats);
- threat model: `freshness` reworded, §1.16 trigger updated, citations moved, validator OK.

Tested:
- `test/status_test.dart` (11 tests):
  - the list: newest per publisher; a status a day or more ahead; untrusted and revoked signers; a revocation, then the earlier replacement, also in one batch; a far-future status in the file; `knownIds`; newer version, last news, and the 30-day hint;
  - `Catalog.parse`: an inline revocation rejects the same catalog's record; an inline withdrawal; known statuses skipped;
  - carriers: a stick carries statuses and the withdrawals inside, and an old stick's far-future status doesn't push the real one out; the base's catalog follows changes.
- `node_test.dart`: kept and passed on with the withdrawals inside; a forged status refused; a far-future one doesn't push it out.
- All pass: app 732, lychnion_node 42, lychnion_format 76. The format package gains `PublisherStatus.deciding` and `maxAhead`.

Self-review: fresh-subagent `code-review-expert --report` (Sonnet), two rounds.

First round: no P0 or P1. Fixed in the second commit:
- P2: after a revocation, the earlier-signed replacement status was not kept.
- P2: `copyToStick` let an old stick's far-future status displace the real one.
- P2: the node and base had no far-future cutoff. It is now shared as `PublisherStatus.deciding`.
- P3:
  - a known status is re-read when its contents were lost;
  - one-pass checking and the scan limits are documented;
  - the quiet card says what its date means, and its icon is decorative;
  - help and CHANGELOG no longer say Lychnion signs a status today.

Second round: no P0 or P1. It confirmed those fixes, and that the base needs no extra cutoff (the list already enforces it). Fixed in the third commit:
- P2: within one batch, a revoked key's later-dated status still hid the real replacement. Statuses are now filtered by trust before the newest is picked.
- P3: two tests used a fixed 2027 "far-future" date that would have broken in six months. They now sign relative to the real clock.
- P3: comments on which statuses are checked again.

Left as they are (P3): a status that carries statements the device does not keep is checked again with each catalog (one check each, within the existing limits). A clock set far back leaves far-ahead statuses out of the next save, and any carrier brings them back.

## Summary

This PR begins the command-recognition slice using mechanics observed in the pinned Pascal `Estado7_Golpes.inc` source:

- facing-relative direction predicates (down/forward/back/up);
- condition atoms and negation;
- ordered AND evaluation;
- button press-edge triggering;
- preserving all matching commands in source rule order;
- down/back hold counters with cap 18 and one-tick decay;
- ordered sequence-state transitions from the matching source state.

## Scope boundary

This is the recognizer foundation, not full fighter command support. Loading each fighter's `[Botoes]` and `[Sequencias]` INI rule data, exact host-button mapping, action dispatch integration, and wiring into `FightSimulation` are still subsequent work. No guessed character mapping is included.

## Tests

Five unit tests cover condition parsing, facing-relative directions, counter cap/decay, ordered multi-match behavior, and sequence transitions. CI runs formatting and `cargo test --all-targets`.

Owner: O&W (head b52d916, on master 9ed2989). K&D E1b part 2, a condition before rings go on.

**While rings are ON:** every 10+ claim says "any 10+ **non-ring** items". `klRingsNote` returns ' non-ring', replacing #76's "(rings not included)". It covers:
- card tier text, count, CTA, modal, bundle hint;
- FAQ question and answer ("any 10 or more non-ring items", "Under 10 non-ring items");
- every delivery line: announce bar, footer, card, sticky, and the `#cartShipVal` note even with no ring in the bag;
- the bag's 70% line + "every non-ring item", progress lines;
- hero "for any 10+ non-ring items" (one line at 375 px);
- meta / JSON-LD / ad line.

**While rings are OFF (today):** byte-for-byte the same as master. The sha pin was re-taken on 9ed2989 (7be61fbc…), and O&W verified it on a clean master worktree.

**Tests:** new `tests/rings-e1c.test.mjs`:
- rings ON: scans EVERY rendered string, and every /10\+|10 or more|any mix/ must carry the note;
- rings OFF: the sha pin.

O&W 295/0/5; App-dev run on the head: see the comment.

**V-01 at 375×812 with rings on:** 1 full card on every tab (the sticky bar is 16 px taller but fits).

**Prod impact today:** none (R disabled).

